WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.240
Fieldnote two. How it comes to life.

2
00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:11.942
How is the stack created,
and when is it usable?

3
00:00:13.406 --> 00:00:16.476
spi up creates the environment.

4
00:00:16.476 --> 00:00:18.886
Flux continues the rollout.

5
00:00:19.676 --> 00:00:24.396
Follow one environment from your
workstation into the resource group,

6
00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:27.126
the cluster, and its namespaces.

7
00:00:27.196 --> 00:00:28.486
Then remove it.

8
00:00:30.268 --> 00:00:34.038
The CLI and Bicep create
Azure and seed the cluster.

9
00:00:34.428 --> 00:00:36.828
Flux assembles the workloads.

10
00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:38.598
Controllers keep them healthy.

11
00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:44.598
Flux starts before the CLI exits, and
both keep working after it returns.

12
00:00:47.032 --> 00:00:50.562
A successful spi up does
not mean the API is ready.

13
00:00:50.712 --> 00:00:55.442
Follow workload health and
initialization with spi status --watch.

14
00:00:55.512 --> 00:00:59.682
Then verify the API path you need,
with an authenticated request.

15
00:01:00.152 --> 00:01:03.682
In this take, the CLI returned
after forty-four minutes.

16
00:01:03.752 --> 00:01:07.442
The rollout reached thirty of
thirty, twenty-three minutes later,

17
00:01:07.512 --> 00:01:09.602
and only then did the lookup answer.

18
00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:17.524
spi down removes compute and data, but keeps
the managed identities and the resource

19
00:01:17.524 --> 00:01:22.094
group. A rebuild reuses the same
names, not the deleted data.

20
00:01:24.608 --> 00:01:28.058
Before the lookup can answer,
follow the same environment.

21
00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:32.138
AKS is created first, in
the stack's resource group.

22
00:01:32.688 --> 00:01:35.418
Common Storage holds the partition table.

23
00:01:36.048 --> 00:01:39.008
The bootstrap seeds
namespaces, configuration,

24
00:01:39.008 --> 00:01:43.098
and identity. Flux rolls
out the Partition service.

25
00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:47.418
Then an API caller's
lookup for opendes works.

26
00:01:47.568 --> 00:01:52.098
It reads stored configuration and never
touches the partition's Cosmos DB,

27
00:01:52.288 --> 00:01:55.018
blob Storage, or Service Bus.

28
00:01:57.244 --> 00:01:58.494
An easy mistake:

29
00:01:58.564 --> 00:02:01.694
GitOps is suspended, so nothing changes.

30
00:02:01.764 --> 00:02:05.534
Before it returns, spi up
suspends Git fetching.

31
00:02:05.684 --> 00:02:07.294
That stops new commits.

32
00:02:07.444 --> 00:02:10.574
But Flux keeps applying the
revision it already has,

33
00:02:10.644 --> 00:02:13.684
so a live edit to a Flux-managed
object is reverted,

34
00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:15.454
and controllers keep running.

35
00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:19.694
To check, read the Git repository
in the Flux namespace.

36
00:02:22.144 --> 00:02:23.354
So now you can say:

37
00:02:23.664 --> 00:02:29.834
the CLI creates, Flux assembles, and
controllers keep workloads healthy.

38
00:02:30.464 --> 00:02:33.914
CLI success is not API readiness.

39
00:02:34.544 --> 00:02:39.994
And teardown removes data, but
preserves identities and names.

40
00:02:42.376 --> 00:02:46.546
Next: where does shared code hand
the lookup to the Azure provider?

